      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To investigate the clinical observations, provider experience, safety, and tolerance of the hysterosalpingo-contrast sonography (HyCoSy) procedure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "A retrospective study design in which data was collected from ninety-six subfertile women who underwent the HyCoSy procedure at the University of Louisville over a 16-month interval."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Ninety-six HyCoSy procedures were performed by a single investigator and contained complete records for review. The authors observed significant decreases in the quantities of saline and air utilized per procedure over time (p < 0.0001 and p = 0.0001). Results from the HyCoSy studies were more often non-diagnostic or non-patent in women with a body mass index (BMI) > 30. Reported pain scores did not statistically differ over the course of the study interval. There were no procedure-related complications noted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The HyCoSy procedure is a timely and minimally invasive study that can be implemented in an office setting with minimal prior operator experience that improves over time."
        }
      ]},
